<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burebista</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/44-bce">44 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burebista was a king of the Getae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> who, from about 82 BCE, united their tribes into a powerful kingdom, aided by the high priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deceneus">Deceneus</a>. He extended his realm from the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, defeating the Boii and Scordisci and sacking Greek cities such as Olbia and Histria. He was murdered in 44 BCE, and his kingdom split apart.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burebista.jpg" alt="Burebista" width="212" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jokes_Free4Me · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burebista_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The area between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> rivers, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, became home to Thracian peoples including the Getae and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> around the 7th and 6th centuries BCE. The Getae lived in the lower Danube basin and traded with the Greek cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, while the Dacians lived along the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a>, whose isolation helped them survive. From the 4th century to the middle of the 2nd century BCE La Tène Celts influenced them, introducing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potters-wheel">potter's wheel</a>, better metalworking and probably coinage. Before Burebista, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> had a succession of kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dromichaetes">Dromichaetes</a>, Oroles and Rubobostes. Only a few ancient sources on Burebista survive, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> and inscriptions from Balchik and Nesebar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The date of his accession is debated among scholars. Burebista was the first king to unify the Dacian tribes, with the support of the religious establishment, and his realm was probably a weakly centralised state with a military organisation like that of the Hellenistic kingdoms; archaeologists disagree about how centralised it was. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orastie-mountains">Orăștie Mountains</a> he built stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> at Costești, Blidaru, Piatra Roșie and Bănița, which show Greek military architecture.</p><p>He destroyed the Boii and Taurisci in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> early in his campaigns, then the Bastarnae, and probably conquered the Scordisci, and he raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Roman Macedonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>. From about 55 BCE he took the Greek cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, among them Olbia, Tyras, Histria, Tomis, Callatis, Odessos, Messembria, Apollonia and Dionysopolis.</p><p>An inscription of 48 BCE calls Akornion of Dionysopolis his friend and says he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> to seek the title king of kings for Burebista. In the Roman civil war Burebista backed Pompey, which made him an enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, who planned a campaign against him. His force may have numbered up to 200,000 men, though it is disputed whether this was an army or all able-bodied men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Caesar was assassinated in 44 BCE, and Burebista was killed at about the same time in a plot by the Dacian aristocracy, who saw a centralised state as a threat to their privileges. His kingdom broke up. It was divided into four parts under the religious elite, and by the time of Augustus into five. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> was united again by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a> and ended as an independent kingdom after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s conquest in 106 CE.</p><p>In the 1960s statues of Burebista and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a> were put up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and in 1980 the government marked the 2,050th anniversary of his state, a commemoration that the Ceaușescu regime used to legitimise its own rule. Historians have described the nationalist claim that Burebista's descendants are the true ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> as an imaginary history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burebista” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1st century BCE the powerful Dacian state of <b>Burebista</b> emerged.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 60 BCE the Boii were crushed by the Dacian king <b>Burebista</b>, and part of the people joined the Helvetii in their failed migration of 58 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/boii">Boii</a></li><li><blockquote>About 82 BCE <b>Burebista</b> united the Getae and Dacians into the Kingdom of Dacia; Roman writers afterwards used Getae and Dacians almost interchangeably.</blockquote><a href="/state/getae">Getae</a></li><li><blockquote>In 72 to 71 BCE Marcus Lucullus brought the western Pontic cities under Roman control, and <b>Burebista's</b> Getae sacked the city soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/histria">Histria</a></li><li><blockquote>About 82 BCE <b>Burebista</b> united the Getae and Dacian tribes, aided by the high priest Deceneus.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dacia">Kingdom of Dacia</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 55 BCE the Getae under <b>Burebista</b> sacked it.</blockquote><a href="/state/olbia">Olbia</a></li></ul></section>
